<!--
    Stack and global blocks on x86-64 Mach-O: the invoke named after its
    creator and typed, the captures and a `__block` cell typed, the creator's
    frame typed, as kuna-objcblocks.xml and kuna-objcblocks2.xml pin for arm64.

    Issue: block recovery read only arm64 creators. On an x86-64 image a stack
    block's invoke stayed a `sub_` taking a `void *`, its captures read as
    offsets off that pointer, the creator was a run of stores to numbered
    locals, and a global block's invoke was `block_invoke_<addr>`.

    Fix: `analyzers/objc/kuna_objcblocks/x86.rs (sites)` decodes each x86-64
    function that references the `_NSConcreteStackBlock` GOT slot or a global
    literal: the isa is `mov r, [rip + slot]`, the invoke and descriptor are
    `lea r, [rip + addr]`, and the stores go through `rbp`, `rsp` or a register
    holding a frame address, at offsets taken relative to `rsp` at entry. The
    copy helper's `objc_retain` and `_Block_object_assign` calls (direct, tail
    or through the GOT) type the captures.

    Pass 1 (`objc off`): `sub_100000a20(void *a0,...)`, the creator stores
    `v6 = sub_100000a20;`. Pass 2 (`objc on`), `macho_objc_blocks_x86_64`:
    `__22-[Runner tally:scale:]_block_invoke(Block_layout_100000a20 *block,...)`
    reading `block->capture_1->forwarding->value`; the creator stores
    `block.block.invoke`, `block.capture_1 = &cell_1`. Pass 3 (`objc on`),
    `macho_objc_blocks2_x86_64`: three blocks of one creator with the isa kept
    in `r12` across the sends, a nested block, a global block, a copy helper.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
